          %p#Idea
          %h2#section_Idea Idea
          %p
            Where a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            may be thought of as a
            %em group of symmetry transformations
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphically
            relates one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            to itself (the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries"} symmetries
            of one object, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometries"} isometries
            of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polyhedron"} polyhedron
            %em groupoid
            is a collection of symmetry transformations acting between possibly more than one object.
          %p
            Hence a groupoid consists of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z, \cdots
            and for each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
            there is a set of transformations, usually denoted by arrows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi f
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} y
          %p which may be composed if they are composable (i.e. if the first ends where the second starts)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi f
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mo ∘
                              %mi f
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; y
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{f}}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{g}}
                  \\\
                  x &amp;&amp; \underset{g \circ f}{\longrightarrow}  &amp;&amp; z
                  }
          %p
            such that this composition is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AssociativityDiagram.png", :width => "400px"}/
          %p
            and such that for each object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            there is identity transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mi X
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \overset{id_X}{\longrightarrow} x
            in that this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+element"} neutral element
            for the composition of transformations, whenever defined.
          %p
            So far this structure is what is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            %em
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+groupoid"}> small
              ) groupoid
            is that all these transformations are to be “symmetries” in that they are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+morphisms"} invertible morphisms
            meaning that for each transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi f
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} y
            there is a transformation the other way around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \overset{f^{-1}}{\longrightarrow} x
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi x
                  %mphantom
                    %mi AAAA
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f^{-1} \circ f  = id_x
                  \\phantom{AAAA}
                  f \circ f^{-1} = id_y
                  \\,.
          %p
            If there is only a single object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            and a choice of one of its objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a group, sometimes called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Aut
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut_{\mathcal{G}}(x)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
          %p
            Just as for groups, the “transformations” above need not necessarily be given by concrete transformations (say by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijections"} bijections
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            which are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            of the groupoid). Generally one calls these “transformations”
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi f
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} y
            is a morphism with “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"}> source
            ”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"}> codomain
            ”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %p
            An archetypical example of a groupoid is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FundamentalGroupoid"}
            below, for introduction see
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Topology+--+2#Homotopy"} here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a topological space, this is the groupoid whose
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                are the points
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \overset{[\gamma]}{\longrightarrow} y
                are the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+relative+boundary"} homotopy relative boundary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\gamma]
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paths"} paths
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \colon [0,1] \to X
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(0) = x
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(1) = y
          %p
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            is given, on representatives, by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concatenation"} concatenation
            of paths. Here the class of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reverse+path"} reverse path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi t
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar\gamma \;\colon\; t \mapsto \gamma(1-t)
            constitutes the inverse morphism, making this a groupoid.
          %p
            If one
            %em chooses
            a point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Aut
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x) \coloneqq Aut_{\Pi_1(X)}(x)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            at that point.
          %p This highlights one of the reasons for being interested in groupoids over groups: Sometimes this allows to avoid unnatural ad-hoc choices and it serves to streamline and simplify the theory.
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            between groupoids is the obvious: a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            between their underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            together with a function between their morphisms which respects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            objects as well as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphism"} group homomorphism
          %p
            For example if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \;\colon\; X \to Y
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            between topological spaces, then postcomposition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paths"} paths
            with this function induces a groupoid homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast \;\colon\; \Pi_1(X) \longrightarrow \Pi_1(Y)
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoids"} fundamental groupoids
            from above.
          %p
            Groupoids with groupoid homomorphisms (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"}> functors
            ) between them form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#1CategoryOfGroupoids"}
            below) which includes the categeory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of the groupoids with a single object. This makes precise how groupoid theory is a generalization of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+theory"} group theory
          %p
            However, for groupoids more than for groups one is typically interested in “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation+actions"}> conjugation actions
            ” on homomorphisms. These are richer for groupoids than for groups, because one may conjugate with a different morphism at each object. If we think of groupoids as special cases of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
          %p
            For examples if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
            are two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \;\colon\; f \Rightarrow g
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+relative+boundary"} homotopy relative boundary
            classes of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paths"} paths
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta(x,-) \;\colon\; [0,1] \to Y
            constitute a natural transformation between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*, g_\ast \;\colon\; \Pi_1(X) \to \Pi_1(Y)
            in that for all paths
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 \overset{[\gamma]}{\longrightarrow} x_2
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            we have the “conjugation relation”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mphantom
                    %mi AAAA
                  %mtext i.e.
                  %mphantom
                    %mi AAAA
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %mi η
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi x
                                %mn 1
                              %mo ,
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mi g
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                %mi f
                                %mo ∘
                                %mi γ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                %mi g
                                %mo ∘
                                %mi γ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %mi η
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi x
                                %mn 2
                              %mo ,
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mi g
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\eta(x_1,-)] \cdot [f\circ\gamma]
                  \=
                  [g \circ \gamma] \cdot [\eta(x_2,-)]
                  \\phantom{AAAA}
                  \\text{i.e.}
                  \\phantom{AAAA}
                  \\array{
                  f(x_1) &amp;\overset{[\eta(x_1,-)]}{\longrightarrow}&amp; g(x_1)
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{[f \circ \gamma]}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{[g \circ \gamma]}}
                  \\\
                  f(x_2) &amp;\underset{[\eta(x_2,-)]}{\longrightarrow}&amp; g(x_2)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p One may take care of the existence of these conjugation actions/natural transformation in two ways:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                If one quotients them out, i.e. if one identifies two groupoid homomorphisms that differ by a conjugation action, then the resulting
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                of groupoids and classes of homomorphisms is called the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Grpd)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
                (def.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyCategoryOfGroupoids"}
                below). This is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+homotopy+category"} classical homotopy category
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                on those that are (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
                and)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"} homotopy 1-types
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
                is the
                %em only
                homotopy invariant, with all higher
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
                being trivial:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Top
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≤
                          %mn 1
                        %mi CW
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi Top
                        %mi CW
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Ho(Grpd) \simeq Ho(Top^{CW}_{\leq 1}) \hookrightarrow Ho(Top^{CW})
                      \\,.
              %p
                This means that the concept of groupoids may be regarded as a combinatorial model for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"} homotopy 1-types
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                given by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+homotopy+theory"} topological homotopy theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-types"} homotopy n-types
                as one passes to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoids"} n-groupoids
                and eventually to all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
                as one passes to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"} infinity-groupoids
            %li
              %p
                Instead of forgetting all the information encoded in the conjugations/natural transformations, one may also collect it all into the structure of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
                (in fact a
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                on those (small) categories all whose morphisms are
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+morphism"} invertible morphism
          %p
            For more introduction on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+homotopy+types"} geometry of physics – homotopy types
          %p
            In either of these two cases, beware that the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            is
            %em not
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            either of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Grpd)
            or of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            it is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            one-object groupoids which are equivalent to groups
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Grp \hookrightarrow Grpd^{\ast/}
                  \\,.
          %p
            (Even though there is a unique choice of point for a one-object groupoid, the respect for this (unique) choice forces the conjugation actions on groupoids to be trivial. ) This simple statement is in fact a special case of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/May+recognition+theorem"} May recognition theorem
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "Ek-Algebras#MainResult"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            and even for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+types"} directed homotopy types
          %p
            The equivalence of groupoids with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"} homotopy 1-types
            shows immediately that, with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            taken into account, the difference between groupoids and groups is rather mild, after all: Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+groupoids"} equivalent
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Grpd)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            of groupoids with a single object (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EveryGroupoidIsEquivalentToDisjointUnionOfGroupDeloopings"}
            below). In particular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+representations"} groupoid representations
            are equivalently just tuples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+representations"} group representations
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupoidRepresentationsAreProductsOfGroupRepresentations"}
            below.) This is the reason why the competition between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+topology"} general topology
            textbooks that do and that do not prefer the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            remains inconclusive.
          %p
            However, this equivalence of groupoids with disjoint unions of groups depends on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          %p
            Just as one may consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groups"} topological groups
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groupoids"} topological groupoids
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            etc. (generally:
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoids"} internal groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"}> higher geometry
            ”).
          %p
            The correct concept of homomorphisms between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            for instance goes by many names, including
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphisms"} Morita morphisms
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilsum-Skandalis+morphisms"} Hilsum-Skandalis morphisms
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bibundles"} bibundles
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
                of groupoids
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            and for introduction in the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+homotopy+types"} geometry of physics – smooth homotopy types
          %p
          %h2#section_Definition Definition
          %p
          %h3#section_Groupoids Groupoids
          #GroupoidHomotopyTypes.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (groupoid –
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopily typed
                definition)
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+groupoid"} small groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              such that for all points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒢
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              in the
