                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+groupoid"} free groupoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(\Gamma)
                is well defined. It is the left
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
                from groupoids to directed graphs. This shows an advantage of groupoids: the notion of
                %em free equivalence relation
                or
                %em free action groupoid
                does not easily make sense. But we can still talk of a presentation of an equivalence relation or action groupoid by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a{:href => "#Zivaljevic"} paper by Živaljević
                gives examples of groupoids used in combinatorics.
            %li
              %p
                The book “Topology and Groupoids” listed below takes the view that 1-dimensional homotopy theory, including the Seifert-van Kampen Theorem, the theory of covering spaces, and the less well known theory of the fundamental group(oid) of an orbit space by a discontinuous group action, is best presented using the notion of groupoid rather than group as basic. This had led in the 1960s to the question of the prospective use of (strict) groupoids in higher homotopy theory. One answer is given in the book
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nonabelian+algebraic+topology"} Nonabelian algebraic topology
          %p#Properties
          %h2#section_Properties Properties
          %p#PropertiesEquivalencesOfGroupoids
          %h3#section_Equivalences_of_groupoids Equivalences of groupoids
          #AutomorphismGroupDependsOnlyOnConnectedComponent.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
                depends on basepoint only up to
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
              a groupoid, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              be two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              in the same
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+component"} connected component
              (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupoidConnectedComponents"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Aut
                      %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Aut
                      %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Aut_{\mathcal{G}}(x) \simeq Aut_{\mathcal{G}}(y)
            %p
              between their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+groups"} automorphism groups
              (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InGrupoidAutomorphismGroup"}
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              By assumption, there exists some morphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi f
                    %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} y
                    \\,.
            %p
              The operation of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
              with this morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Aut
                              %mi 𝒢
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi Ad
                                  %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Aut
                              %mi 𝒢
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi f
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi g
                            %mo ∘
                            %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    Aut_{\mathcal{G}}(x)
                    \&amp;\overset{Ad_{f}}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    Aut_{\mathcal{G}}(y)
                    \\\
                    g &amp;\mapsto&amp; f^{-1} \circ g \circ f
                    }
            %p is clearly a group isomorphism as required.
          #DeloopingGroupoidEquivalence.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_4 Lemma
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+groupoids"} equivalences
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} disjoint unions
                of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping+groupoids"} delooping groupoids
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{G_i\}_{i \in I}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{H_j\}_{j \in J}
              be sets of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
              and consider a homomorphism (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"}> functor
              )
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⟶
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\underset{i \in I}{\sqcup} G_i
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\underset{j \in J}{\sqcup} H_j
            %p
              between the corresponding disjoint unions of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping+groupoids"} delooping groupoids
              (example
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupoidFromDelooping"}
            %p Then the following are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+groupoids"} equivalence of groupoids
                  (def.
                  = succeed ");" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EquivalenceOfGroupoids"}
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
                  (def.
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupoidWeakHomotopyEquivalence"}
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              The implication 2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow 1)
              is immediate.
            %p
              In the other direction, assume that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is an equivalence of groupoids, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              be an inverse up to natural isomorphism. It is clear that both induces bijections on connected components. To see that both are isomorphisms of automorphisms groups, observe that the conditions for the natural isomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
